    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1259">

                <pb facs="1297" n="1285" sameAs="frus:frus1945v08/pg1285"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.67/12–2145: Circular telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        Certain Diplomatic Representatives in Europe and
                        the Near East</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-12-21T15:00:00-05:00">December 21,
                            1945—3 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Soviet Consulates in Turkey, Iran and probably elsewhere are registering persons
                    of Armenian origin who wish to go to Soviet Armenia which according to reports
                    is unable to support a greatly increased population. The artificial population
                    problem thus created may reinforce demands for Turkey’s eastern provinces.
                    Please report developments in your country as regards number and type of
                    Armenians registering with Soviet authorities and departing for USSR.<anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d1259-f1"/> Repeat data to Ankara, Moscow and London.</p>
                <p>Sent to Ankara, Moscow, London, Paris, Beirut for Damascus also, Cairo and
                    Baghdad.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1259-f1" n="5" xml:id="d1259-f1-content">
                <p>A few early reports received by the Department by the end of the year gave
                    conflicting and imprecise information.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
